WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:06.970
ALEX GOLD: Thanks so
much for tuning in today

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:12.320
to talk about managing packages
for open-source data science.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.790
My name is Alex, and I'm a
solutions engineering manager

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.920
here at RStudio.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.620
Just a little about me
so that you know who I am

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.600
and why I'm talking
about this, I'm

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.690
a former data scientist and
a data-science team lead.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.940
I worked on health
care, politics,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.910
economic policy kinds of issues
when I was a full-time data

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:31.450
scientist.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.370
And since coming
to RStudio, I've

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.740
been a solutions engineer
here for about two years.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.080
So that's me.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.650
So let's dive right in and
talk about managing packages.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:46.758
So data science is a lot
like this delicious sandwich.

00:00:46.758 --> 00:00:48.550
This sandwich looks
incredible, by the way.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.230
If you know how to
make this sandwich,

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.575
let me know, because wow.

00:00:53.575 --> 00:00:56.630
Anyway, data science is a
little like making a delicious

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:57.130
sandwich.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.690
Maybe it's an app
that you're making.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:02.380
Maybe it's an API or a port or
a scheduled job of some sort.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.900
And it takes a little bit of
planning, a little preparation,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.680
and a little skill to make
something really, really great.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.590
Today, we're going to talk
about one important aspect

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.640
of the open-source data science,
which is package management.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.490
And so we'll start talking
about how package management can

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.340
screw up your plans to
make beautiful, delicious

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.670
sandwiches, or data-science
products, some background

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.920
on how it all works,
and then, finally, some

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.040
thoughts on how it can be
better and how it doesn't

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.440
have to always be so painful.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.800
So let's dive right in.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.210
So first, the pain.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.680
So one reason this
can be painful

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.073
is that folks are
blocked, right?

00:01:40.073 --> 00:01:41.740
You're blocked from
installing packages.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.600
Either you don't
have permissions

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.513
to install on your
machine, or you're

00:01:46.513 --> 00:01:48.430
blocked from public
package sources like CRAN,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.380
Bioconductor, or PyPI--

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.760
and that's a big problem--

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.250
or you have no way to
share private packages.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.860
And I will say that there
are some organizations that

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.420
have bans against installing
stuff to your own machine.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.070
And there's sort of
no way around that.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.740
But hopefully what you can come
out of this presentation with

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.210
is some information on how
to work with your IT team

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.280
to get to a situation to
do the work that you really

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.370
need to do.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.880
And that leads into the
situation of it feeling like IT

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.950
and data science are at odds.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.450
And this isn't so
surprising, right?

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.850
Data scientists want
the software they want,

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.340
and they want it
when they want it.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.888
And that's not necessarily
always IT's concern, right?

00:02:29.888 --> 00:02:31.930
IT or admins have a lot
of concern about platform

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.210
stability and security.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.010
And those things don't have to
be at odds, but they can be.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.070
And so that can be a problem.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.400
There's the issue of fragility.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.685
When you're sharing your
project with somebody else,

00:02:44.685 --> 00:02:46.060
are you confident
that they'll be

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.280
able to open it up
and actually use it?

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.020
Or might they open
it up and find

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.437
themselves stuck because they
don't have the right package

00:02:52.437 --> 00:02:53.390
set?

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.000
And then, lastly,
when you come back

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.100
to a project in the
future, this is just a form

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.490
of sharing with future you.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.630
But how confident are you
that your package set hasn't

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.240
changed in the meantime and you
can get back to the same state?

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.360
And so to be
completely clear, you

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.800
can't fix all these problems.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.720
And particularly, if you're
just a lone data scientist,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.387
it may not be possible
to fix all these.

00:03:12.387 --> 00:03:15.130
You may need some help from
others at your organization.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:18.890
But you can set yourself
up for success here.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.970
And so let's talk a
little bit about how.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:22.922
I love to cook.

00:03:22.922 --> 00:03:24.505
And when you're
talking about cooking,

00:03:24.505 --> 00:03:25.880
there's this
concept called "mise

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.772
en place," which means
"putting in place" in French.

00:03:28.772 --> 00:03:29.980
That's just my understanding.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.780
I don't speak French.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.620
But what it's really about
is, you cup, measure, and prep

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.150
all of your ingredients before
you ever turn on your stove,

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:38.650
right?

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.680
So by the time you're
turning on the gas,

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.960
by the time you're
starting to cook,

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.410
everything is in place and
ready to go by that time.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.680
And so one of the reasons that--

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.050
you can't guarantee
outcomes, but you can really

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.310
guarantee your process.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.387
And so that's what the
mise-en-place step is meant

00:03:58.387 --> 00:03:59.470
to do when you're cooking.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.750
And you can do something
very similar, right?

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.800
Package management is very
much like the mise en place

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.233
of doing data science, right?

00:04:07.233 --> 00:04:08.650
It's getting all
your ingredients,

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:11.260
all your things in
place before you go.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.690
So let's take a step back
and talk a little bit about,

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:16.100
how do the ingredients get
there in the first place?

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.338
And how do you make
sure they're ready to go

00:04:18.338 --> 00:04:19.630
when you're doing data science?

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:21.297
We're going to stick
with this metaphor.

00:04:21.297 --> 00:04:23.800
So your food starts at
the grocery store, right?

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:24.700
It's packaged up.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:25.460
It's on the shelf.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:26.168
It's ready to go.

00:04:26.168 --> 00:04:27.160
And you go and get it.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.930
And then when you want
to cook something,

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.472
you go and buy the
right ingredients,

00:04:30.472 --> 00:04:32.410
and you bring them home
to your pantry, right?

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.025
There's this shopping process.

00:04:34.025 --> 00:04:35.650
You go and you fill
your shopping cart.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.370
You pay for it.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:37.430
You take it home.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:40.610
And it's very
similar for packages.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.630
So that's the
shopping thing where

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:47.170
the stuff gets from the
grocery store to your pantry.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.390
And it's really the
same for packages.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.700
For packages, your grocery
store is your repository.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.050
And so this is an
external place where

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.090
you have all of your
packages, and they're

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.020
stored, ready for you
to go in an inert way.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:03.700
And then there's your library.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.350
And that is your
pantry where you

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.960
keep your packages that are
just for you and the things

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.330
you're going to make.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.950
And that's how that works.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.760
And so for many teams, the
repository that they use

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.740
is public, right?

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.440
It might be public CRAN, public
Bioconductor, public package

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:21.580
manager.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.730
Other teams choose to maintain
private repositories--

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.010
RStudio Package Manager and
miniCRAN, something else.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:28.720
That's really fine.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.910
But the libraries
are always private.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.220
And the libraries are
anywhere there's a kitchen,

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.550
anywhere there's actual R
or Python processes running

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.412
in the IDE, in a notebook,
on RStudio Connect,

00:05:40.412 --> 00:05:41.620
in a Docker container, right?

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.020
There's a library
attached to that.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.030
And the shopping process
is the install, right?

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.630
So the packages get from
the grocery-store repository

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:55.120
into your private pantry
library via the install.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.458
And just to be a little
more precise about this,

00:05:59.458 --> 00:06:01.000
I have these two
sorted side by side,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.580
but really the way this works
is that like the grocery store,

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:08.350
and pantries, many libraries
are served by one repository,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:08.920
right?

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:13.660
One repository provides
packages for many libraries.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:16.770
And so let's dive in a
little bit on how that works.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.270
So a repository is
just a file server.

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:22.738
It has a bunch of directories
indexed in a particular way.

00:06:22.738 --> 00:06:25.030
And so just to be precise
[INAUDIBLE] this is the link.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.350
You'll note it's a URL, right?

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:29.440
It's a real link to an internet
site for a particular package

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:30.610
on CRAN, right?

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:35.200
Now, CRAN is the main R package
repository-- public one.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.290
And so what you'll see here
is, this is for Windows.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.240
It is using the
development version of R--

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:41.980
R version 4.1.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.653
It is for the A3
package, which I

00:06:43.653 --> 00:06:45.820
chose because it is the
alphabetically first package

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:46.840
on CRAN.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.610
And it's version 1.0.0.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:52.750
And so literally, this is
the directory structure

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.820
inside the CRAN repository.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:57.850
And so this is just
a directory that's

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.180
organized in this
particular way that

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:02.860
R-- when you go to
install a package,

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.920
it knows how to walk
this tree and get

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.723
the package in the package
versions that you need.

00:07:09.723 --> 00:07:11.890
One of the other important
aspects of the repository

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.050
is that the repository
is not local.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.070
It is almost never the
case that your repository

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.970
is on the same machine
as where you're actually

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:19.642
doing your work.

00:07:19.642 --> 00:07:21.100
Very often, the
repository is going

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.990
to be actually in the cloud.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.600
It's actually going to be a
website, like that URL I just

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:26.890
showed you.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:30.670
Or, sometimes, it's going to be
an internal networked server.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.590
But usually, even
if you're doing

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.260
work on, like, RStudio
Server, it's almost never

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.120
going to be on the same server.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.040
It's going to be a
different server.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.110
That's very much in
contrast to the library.

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.120
The library is just a directory.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.210
But it's on the same machine
as the running R process.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:49.690
And so you'll see,
this is a Linux path

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.940
to inside my home directory.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:56.800
It's the R x86,
64 Linux library.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.780
And you'll see, this is
specific to R version

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:01.780
4.1 and the A3 package.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.640
And so one of the
big differences

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:07.480
here is that libraries are
specific to the version of R.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:09.370
They are not specific
to the package version.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.840
And this is because R needs a
way to resolve, definitively,

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.250
to a particular package.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.780
And in particular,
the thing that

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.837
arises here is, some
people wonder, oh,

00:08:19.837 --> 00:08:21.920
why couldn't you just have
a package version here?

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.860
And the reason is that, then,
when A3 has dependencies

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.860
that it needs to go get,
it needs to be able to,

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:28.930
at some level, fetch
those by name, right?

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.420
It needs to just get
the version that's there

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:33.940
and not maintain many
versions at once.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:36.610
And so this is one of
the big distinctions

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:38.770
between the repository
and the library,

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.840
is that the library
has, at most,

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:42.580
one version of any package.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.280
The repository has many
versions of each package,

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.450
and even many versions for
different operating systems.

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:54.430
And in either case, the thing
that ties it all together

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:56.440
is your settings on
your machine, right?

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.970
That tells your R or Python
process what repository to use,

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:02.750
what library to use--
that sort of thing.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.583
And so one thing I want
to make really clear

00:09:04.583 --> 00:09:06.867
is that this is the
same for R and Python.

00:09:06.867 --> 00:09:08.950
There are some minor
differences in tooling, which

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.250
we'll get to in just a moment.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.190
But everything I've talked about
so far, and most everything I'm

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.500
going to talk about for
the rest of this webinar,

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.840
is identical in R and Python.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:20.380
The conceptual way that
repositories and libraries

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:25.330
house packages is exactly
the same in R and Python.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.430
So let's get a little
into the nitty-gritty

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:30.940
here of how these
package installs actually

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.950
work in R and Python.

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.140
So let's say I go to
install a package.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.910
In R, I do that with
install.packages.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:39.040
In Python, I do that
with pip install package.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.210
And you'll notice, here is
one of the differences, which

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:46.005
is that, in R, I do all of
my actual package management

00:09:46.005 --> 00:09:48.790
and installation
from inside R, right?

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:52.300
I type install.packages at
a running R interpreter.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.590
That's different in Python.

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.500
In Python, you use
a separate binary

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.810
to manage your package installs.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:00.880
Most often, that's pip.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.460
It can also be
something like conda.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:07.520
But either way, it's a separate
thing from your running Python

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:08.020
process.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:09.520
You don't do this in
a Python interpreter.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.853
You do this at the command line.

00:10:10.853 --> 00:10:12.880
And so that's a
difference in tooling.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.490
But in terms of
what these tools do,

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:17.270
they are conceptually identical.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.062
So in either case, what
they're going to do

00:10:19.062 --> 00:10:20.830
is they're going to
look at your settings.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.890
In particular, in R, it's going
to look at your options repos.

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:26.650
In Python, it's going to
look at your pip config.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:30.475
And from there, it's going to
find the URL of a repository.

00:10:30.475 --> 00:10:32.350
And remember, this is
going to be a real URL,

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:36.602
either on the internet
or a networked server.

00:10:36.602 --> 00:10:38.560
And from there, it's
going to move that package

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.500
into your particular library.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:44.335
Now, when I go to
actually load a package,

00:10:44.335 --> 00:10:46.270
in either R or
Python, I do that in R

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.370
with the library
package command.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.355
In Python, I do it
with import package.

00:10:50.355 --> 00:10:52.480
And so what I'm going to
do from there is I'm going

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.450
to-- or, what R or Python are
going to do [INAUDIBLE] going

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:58.360
to look at the-- in R, the
.libPaths, or in Python,

00:10:58.360 --> 00:10:58.902
the sys.path.

00:10:58.902 --> 00:11:01.068
Hopefully, you've never had
to look at these before,

00:11:01.068 --> 00:11:02.950
because they just
resolve automatically.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.620
From that, R and Python will
know which library to look to.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:08.630
And we'll bring that back
into your running R or Python

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:09.130
environment.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:12.630
And then you can use the
functions from those packages.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.180
So this was a lot of
nitty-gritty, in-depth stuff.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:19.678
And if this all
felt like this was

00:11:19.678 --> 00:11:21.970
really exciting and intriguing
to you, congratulations.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:24.220
You're a huge nerd, and
you're in great company.

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:26.450
So delighted to have you here.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:29.170
But if this went over
your head a little bit

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.270
or didn't feel that
important, that's great.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:32.443
That's totally fine.

00:11:32.443 --> 00:11:34.360
You should never need
to manage this directly.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.845
But I share all this
mainly to make the point

00:11:36.845 --> 00:11:39.220
that you can see why this is
a little bit frustrating why

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:43.873
there's no single silver
bullet to make this all work.

00:11:43.873 --> 00:11:45.790
Successfully managing
your package environment

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:49.230
requires at least three
different components, right?

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.870
It requires the repository, the
library, and the settings that

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:54.130
tie it all together, right?

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:56.350
All three of those
things need to work.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.380
And so there's no
single silver bullet

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.870
to make this all just work.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.810
That said, there
is a happy path.

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.390
It is possible to make this
delicious sandwich, whatever

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:08.620
it is.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.210
And it's not even that hard.

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:12.880
But you're going to want to
do some planning up front.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.130
You're going to want to
do your mise en place.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:16.810
And so we're going
to talk, for the rest

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.880
of this talk, about two ways
that you can make this better.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:23.260
And the first is to develop
a package-management plan

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.360
with your IT department.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:29.320
And the second is [INAUDIBLE]
project-specific libraries

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.510
that you can manage as an
individual data scientist.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:33.010
And so from here,
we're going to get

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.513
to the package-management
prime directive, which

00:12:35.513 --> 00:12:36.430
is a little grandiose.

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:40.100
But I like this
slide so [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:42.190
And so the first is
that repositories should

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:43.660
be as broad as possible, right?

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.580
These should be
managed centrally,

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.200
if they're being managed at all.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:51.040
And they should try and serve
as many users as possible.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.050
That is the opposite from
libraries, which are specific.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:56.050
One other thing I should
mention about libraries

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:57.500
is that they are
pull only, right?

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:59.770
You have to pull packages
to your libraries.

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:02.290
And so that means that
the data scientist

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:05.470
should have control of what they
pull into their own libraries.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.350
This is sometimes a point
of friction between IT teams

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:10.420
and data scientists.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:13.690
And it's worth
having a conversation

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:17.405
with your IT team about how
you can safely manage libraries

00:13:17.405 --> 00:13:19.030
as the data-science
team, because we've

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:22.520
see many, many people doing
it quite successfully.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:25.095
So to dive in a
little bit on that,

00:13:25.095 --> 00:13:26.470
who manages each
of these things?

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:29.503
So on your repository,
you don't necessarily

00:13:29.503 --> 00:13:30.670
need to manage a repository.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:33.490
A lot of organizations choose
not to manage a repository.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.610
And we'll get, in a minute,
into why you might want to.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.880
But many organizations choose
not to manage a repository.

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:41.710
If you are going to
manage one, that's

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:43.330
generally on IT or admins.

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.550
And sometimes you
have data scientists

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:49.150
serving in this role of the
data-science platform admin.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:49.817
And that's fine.

00:13:49.817 --> 00:13:51.483
But it's good to
distinguish what you're

00:13:51.483 --> 00:13:53.440
doing as a data-science
platform admin

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:56.460
versus as a data scientist.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:58.380
And so, most often,
we see IT admins

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.660
managing a repository,
because the job here

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.953
is to stand up a server, to
network it correctly, to make

00:14:03.953 --> 00:14:05.370
sure that it's
available to users,

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:07.440
to make sure that it stays
up, has the appropriate amount

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:08.730
of stability and security.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:11.040
And so these are
really IT admin tasks

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.380
in terms of managing
a repository.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:16.140
On the library side, that really
falls to the data scientist--

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.690
to manage either a user-level
library or, increasingly,

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:20.760
and as I'm going to
suggest to do here,

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:22.710
a project-level library, right?

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:26.118
An individual library that
corresponds to a single project

00:14:26.118 --> 00:14:27.660
is the safest path,
because then, you

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.368
know that, even if
you do other projects,

00:14:29.368 --> 00:14:32.513
you won't have to worry about
breaking your project library.

00:14:32.513 --> 00:14:34.680
And then the settings that
tie them together, right?

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:37.410
If you're on a server,
like RStudio Server,

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:39.840
this most often is administered
by the IT or the admin.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:41.548
If you're on the
desktop, this is usually

00:14:41.548 --> 00:14:46.010
administered by the
individual data scientist.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:50.227
And so then the question
is, how do you manage this?

00:14:50.227 --> 00:14:51.560
What are the tools that you use?

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:55.160
So on the repository
level, obviously, we

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:57.410
recommend using RStudio
Package Manager as the package

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:01.250
repository for CRAN,
Bioconductor, and PyPI

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:03.560
repositories.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.630
There are also open-source
products like miniCRAN

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:08.150
that you can use, if that's
what your organization or you

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:09.110
prefer.

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:12.733
On the library side,
there's renv and virtualenv.

00:15:12.733 --> 00:15:14.900
And this is another difference
between R and Python.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:20.570
In R land, the two options
that most people use to do this

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.430
are either Packrat or renv.

00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:24.560
Renv is a full
replacement for Packrat

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.700
and, in fact, was written
by the same person.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:28.610
So if you're using
Packrat, upgrade to renv.

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:30.818
If you're not managing
project [INAUDIBLE] libraries,

00:15:30.818 --> 00:15:31.490
start doing it.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:33.020
Use renv.

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:35.550
On the Python side,
there are a ton

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:36.918
of options for how to do this.

00:15:36.918 --> 00:15:37.710
There's virtualenv.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:38.310
There's conda.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:39.120
There's pyenv.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.450
There's all these
different things.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.940
And if your organization
has a standard, that's fine.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.380
Use what you want to use.

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.123
But if you aren't
using anything yet,

00:15:49.123 --> 00:15:50.790
I would encourage you
to use virtualenv.

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.070
It's relatively simple.

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:56.490
And as far as I've seen, I've
seen lots of organizations

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.110
successfully use virtualenv.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.260
And so that's the one that
I recommend most often.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.090
And in terms of
the settings, that

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:05.310
can be configured
inside the RStudio IDE,

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.350
if you're using the RStudio IDE.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:11.130
Or you can use config files
like Rprofile or Rprofile.site

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:16.590
or Renviron or Renviron.site
to set those settings.

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:17.090
Great.

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:20.200
So let's talk a little bit
about why organizations want

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.080
to manage private repositories.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:27.130
And the main reason
here is that when

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:29.590
IT needs to control
the set of packages

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:32.330
that's available to people,
this is how you do it.

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:34.960
And I want to emphasize
this, that if you are

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:38.530
in an organization where
you cannot install things--

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.930
install R packages
on your machine--

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:42.547
you're in a broken
state, and you

00:16:42.547 --> 00:16:44.380
need to figure out a
way to convince your IT

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:45.630
team to let you install stuff.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:49.480
It's really, really hard to
work in that environment.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:51.820
One answer is to get
a centralized server.

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:56.590
But the other answer is to
allow installs only from a Py

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:57.860
private repository, right?

00:16:57.860 --> 00:16:59.410
So that's [INAUDIBLE]
see people standing up

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:00.190
private repositories.

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:02.357
And that is because they're
in offline or air-gapped

00:17:02.357 --> 00:17:03.150
situations.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.665
That is, there's no access
to the internet from where

00:17:05.665 --> 00:17:06.790
you're installing packages.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:09.349
Now, that's a
common sort of thing

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:10.599
in high-security environments.

00:17:10.599 --> 00:17:11.750
And that's totally fine.

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:14.230
The solution is to
have a repository

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:17.800
behind your firewall,
and then allow people

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.020
free and open access
to that repository

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:22.089
and lock them from
using any repository

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:23.650
outside the firewall.

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:25.690
Similarly, if you have
to validate package sets

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:27.940
into your environment, if
that's why you can't install

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:30.100
packages-- because you
can't install anything--

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:32.020
again, the way to
solve this problem

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.480
is to have the repository
be the gateway, right?

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:37.300
The repository chooses what
are the sets of packages

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.258
people are allowed to install
for safety or security reasons.

00:17:40.258 --> 00:17:41.800
And then what they
can install-- this

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:43.960
can also work really well
if you have projects that

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:46.268
require package validation.

00:17:46.268 --> 00:17:48.310
And then once those packages
have been validated,

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:49.843
you can only use that set.

00:17:49.843 --> 00:17:51.760
This is a great way to
administer that, right?

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:54.640
You administer-- the IT
or admin administers it

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.227
at the repository level.

00:17:56.227 --> 00:17:58.060
And then data scientists
are free to install

00:17:58.060 --> 00:18:01.215
whatever packages are available
to them from the repository.

00:18:01.215 --> 00:18:02.590
But they can't
get anything else.

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:04.780
All they can get is
what's in the repository.

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:07.270
And then the last piece
here is, if you're

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:11.230
doing private internal packages
that you're developing locally,

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:15.610
having an offline repository
can be a great way to do it.

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:17.230
So for the rest of
this presentation,

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:18.688
we're going to talk
about how to do

00:18:18.688 --> 00:18:20.103
your mise en place with renv.

00:18:20.103 --> 00:18:22.020
And we're going to talk
about some of the ways

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:23.760
that renv can be helpful here.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:26.430
And that is by
isolating your libraries

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.900
on a per-project
basis, which we've

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.000
been talking about
being a good thing,

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:33.760
using a snapshotted repository
from RStudio Package Manager

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:37.240
to ensure
reproducibility later on,

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.400
sharing or restoring
a project when you're

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:42.067
sharing with a colleague or when
you're coming back to a project

00:18:42.067 --> 00:18:44.370
after some time, and how
to start a new project

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:47.770
and easily do your mise
en place to make sure

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:51.110
that this works in a way
that you're happy with, too.

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:54.370
So first, let's open up
this very ominously named

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:58.190
package_fails project here.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.820
And so what I want
to point out is,

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:04.060
this is some code in
an R Markdown document.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:07.612
It last ran in 2018, in June.

00:19:07.612 --> 00:19:09.820
I'm not going to get into
really what this code does.

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:11.445
And so what I'll say
upfront, actually,

00:19:11.445 --> 00:19:13.300
is, what this code
does is pretty silly.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:15.220
So please don't judge
me based on that.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:17.553
What I wanted to do is I
wanted to find an example where

00:19:17.553 --> 00:19:21.070
upgrading the package versions
