00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:04.020
this is my user-level library.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:05.760
And this is the
system-level library.

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:10.093
So at some point, in my
user libraries since 2018,

00:22:10.093 --> 00:22:11.760
I've updated the
version of tidyr, which

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:14.700
is not very hard to imagine.

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:17.790
On the other hand, inside
this version that works,

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.550
the libPaths point to--

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:21.760
this is my home directory.

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:23.520
So this is inside
the renv_working.

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:28.720
Inside this project,
there's an renv library

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:33.250
that is going to be keeping this
package safe for me for later.

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:34.300
So that's great.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:35.830
Renv has done its job.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:37.520
I am keeping myself safe.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:39.490
So that solves one
problem, right?

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:41.680
My package versions will
stay consistent as long

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:46.900
as I keep this renv
project with this project.

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:49.860
So what happens, now, if I want
to install another package?

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:53.080
Let's say I want
to install ggplot.

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:56.155
Now, I did install
package ggplot.

00:22:56.155 --> 00:22:56.800
Oh, great.

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:59.180
So it installed ggplot 2.2.1.

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:02.750
So that's great because ggplot
2.2.1, I happen to know,

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:06.650
is the version from June 2018.

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:10.490
Currently, ggplot2 is
on 3.3.3, which is not

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:11.810
the version I want, right?

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:12.530
That's too new.

00:23:12.530 --> 00:23:14.520
It might break.

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.390
So how did I get ggplot2?

00:23:17.390 --> 00:23:20.840
And of course, if I look
at my options repos, that's

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:25.940
the answer, is that my repo here
is pointed at a public RStudio

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:27.320
package manager.

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:29.750
And I happen to know that
this is the URL format.

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:31.458
That means that this
is a dated repo.

00:23:31.458 --> 00:23:33.000
And so let's look
at what that means.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.240
So here is RStudio
Package Manager.

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:36.980
This is public RStudio
Package Manager--

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.290
packagemanager.rstudio.com.

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:42.740
You can go there and
use it as you wish.

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:46.440
It's free and available to use.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:50.400
And so what I'll see is that,
if I go here to the Setup pane,

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:51.260
I can get--

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:57.680
if I go back to June 2018,
there's that 72, right?

00:23:57.680 --> 00:23:58.680
That is from that date.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:02.000
So what that means-- that
every time I install packages

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:06.395
from this repository, they will
work together as of June 2018.

00:24:06.395 --> 00:24:08.270
And so I never have to
worry about installing

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:09.978
a new version that's
going to mess things

00:24:09.978 --> 00:24:12.130
up, because they will
always play nicely together.

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:13.400
The other thing I will
say-- and this is, again,

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:15.650
a thing that's different
between R and Python--

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:19.310
is that, in R, you
need to specify

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:22.250
that you want binary
repositories for your operating

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:24.440
system [INAUDIBLE] to
the operating system,

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:27.590
and you can change that here
to choose the right repository.

00:24:27.590 --> 00:24:30.470
This is really important,
just because using

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:32.750
binary repositories can
really, really speed up

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:35.670
install times by, like,
several orders of magnitude.

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:39.590
And so it's worth it to
choose a binary repository.

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:41.450
That's not necessary for Python.

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:43.760
Python, by default,
will always give you

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:47.820
the binaries for your system.

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:48.320
So anyway.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:48.820
So OK.

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:52.760
So now we know how I got this--

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.590
how this package
worked, that sort

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:58.010
of thing, why my tidyr works.

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.140
But how about, how is
this being maintained?

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:02.630
And so what R does
is R [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:02.630 --> 00:25:05.420
what's called a lock file.

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:09.320
And so this lock file contains
my version of R, right?

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:11.960
It says it's R version 3.6.2.

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:13.610
This has that
repository in it, right?

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:15.140
That's that repository
that I used.

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:19.740
And so that-- ooh,
zoomed out too much.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.020
This is that repository
that I had set

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.280
when I started this project.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:25.260
And then I have a list
of all of my packages,

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:27.510
what version they are, what
repository they're using.

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:30.492
If I have GitHub packages,
that will record that as well.

00:25:30.492 --> 00:25:32.700
Or, sorry, Git packages in
general-- not just GitHub,

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:34.270
but also GitLab, GitBucket.

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:36.020
And so this gets
recorded in my lock file.

00:25:36.020 --> 00:25:38.460
And so if I want to
make another project--

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:42.990
let's say I want to share
this with my colleagu--

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:47.430
all I need to do is I need
to share the renv lock

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:48.070
file with them.

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:48.600
So here's what I'm going to do.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.430
I'm going to take
the renv lock file,

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:53.100
and I'm going to copy it
to the broken version.

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:56.820
So all that I've done--

00:25:56.820 --> 00:25:59.670
this is the version of this
project that does not work.

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:02.280
Tidyr is wrong here, right?

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.860
This is-- actually, I've
got to load the data.

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:06.630
And tidyr does not
work here, right?

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:08.340
This is, again,
that wrong version

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:10.553
of tidyr, the new
version of tidyr.

00:26:10.553 --> 00:26:11.970
And so now what
I'm going to do is

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:13.890
I'm going to do renv
init, which will

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:16.350
stand up my renv virtual
environment [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:17.100
this project.

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:20.462
And it will restore it, in
particular, from the lock file.

00:26:20.462 --> 00:26:22.170
The other thing I just
want you to notice

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:24.900
is, that was almost
instantaneous in terms

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:26.940
of restoring that lock file.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:31.860
And the reason is because renv
maintains a user-level cache.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.187
So if I've ever installed
these packages before,

00:26:34.187 --> 00:26:35.520
they automatically get restored.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:36.780
And it is super fast.

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:38.070
And so now, if I go--

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:43.200
you'll see package
version tidyr 0.8.1.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:43.740
Awesome.

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:45.330
Now I've got the right version.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:48.450
Now I can run this code,
and it just works, right?

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:51.000
So this is really awesome,
and it makes an easy way

00:26:51.000 --> 00:27:00.390
to share a project and an
environment with somebody else,

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:02.610
or with future you.

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:03.110
Great.

00:27:03.380 --> 00:27:05.505
So one more question that
comes up all the time is,

00:27:05.505 --> 00:27:08.900
OK, great [INAUDIBLE] but how
do I do this for a new project,

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:09.660
right?

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:10.790
How do I start this afresh?

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:12.620
And so it's really quite easy.

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:15.560
When I go to New Project
here in the RStudio IDE,

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:16.640
New Directory--

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:18.410
I'm going to start
a new project.

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:21.710
I'm going to call
this my_new_project.

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:24.410
And now all I do is I'm
going to click here,

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:27.720
"use renv with this project."

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:31.610
And now renv is going to
be set up with this project

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:35.090
as it starts here
on RStudio Server.

00:27:35.090 --> 00:27:38.150
It's going to set up a new
project for me with my folder

00:27:38.150 --> 00:27:40.850
there with my R Proj file--
that's the project folder--

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:45.080
and an renv library that
is attached to the project

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:49.870
by default, which is really
a nice thing to do here.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:53.800
Now, so what this means
is that, whenever--

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:56.140
if I go to install packages--

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:00.920
if I could spell it
right, that would help--

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:03.770
it will install-- again, it
linked in from that cache

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:05.630
super, super quick.

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:07.550
And so that's the
part of using renv.

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:09.750
Now, what if I want to use
a snapshotted repository?

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:11.417
So there's a little
bit of judgment here

00:28:11.417 --> 00:28:13.130
around what the
date is to choose.

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:15.740
You want to choose a date
that works for your project.

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:17.360
My general recommendation
is to choose

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:20.522
a date that's around the time
your product is pretty stable.

00:28:20.522 --> 00:28:21.980
So you probably
don't want it to be

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:23.280
the day you start the project.

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:25.430
But maybe when the bulk
of the coding is done,

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:27.890
and you're thinking, OK, it's
time to save this for later,

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:31.630
you can do renv
modify and go in here.

00:28:31.630 --> 00:28:34.220
Oh, here's my renv lock file.

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:35.570
And I can edit.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:37.550
Let's get a repository
that's more up to date.

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:40.420
Let's say I want
to do yesterday.

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:44.860
I can get that lock file--

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:46.030
the right repository.

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:48.020
I just put it in here.

00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:52.650
And now when I
start a new session,

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:55.170
it's going to automatically
set my repository

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.506
to be the right repository.

00:28:57.506 --> 00:29:01.100
It's going to take just
a second here to reload.

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:02.690
But after it reloads,
what you'll see

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:08.810
is that it's going to be that
updated version of the package

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:10.010
manager repository.

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:12.585
Now if I want to say--

00:29:12.585 --> 00:29:14.210
one other thing just
to be aware of is,

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:18.740
the way to capture dependencies
is, I'm going to save--

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:20.180
let's save an R file here.

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:23.540
And now when I do
renv snapshot, it's

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:26.480
going to introspect
on those files.

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:29.240
And it's realized that
I need ggplot2 in here

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:32.140
and all of its dependencies.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:34.140
So this makes it
really easy to use.

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:36.210
You can also set
up auto snapshots

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:39.470
so that it will snapshot any
time you type install.packages

00:29:39.470 --> 00:29:42.160
inside this project folder.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:42.660
Great.

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:44.180
So that's basically
what I wanted

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:47.090
to share in terms of using renv.

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:49.260
And so we're going
to wrap up now.

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:53.610
And so just a couple of
things to review here.

00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:56.780
So the thing you want
to do is-- two things

00:29:56.780 --> 00:29:58.202
to encourage you to do.

00:29:58.202 --> 00:30:02.510
Do a package-management
plan with your group, right?

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:04.035
How are you managing
repositories?

00:30:04.035 --> 00:30:05.660
What are those settings
supposed to be?

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:08.090
The default repos.

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:10.160
And how are you
managing libraries?

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:12.650
And before you start your
individual data-science work,

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:14.360
do your mise en place.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:15.545
Use renv.

00:30:15.545 --> 00:30:17.510
And I just showed
off how to use renv

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:20.240
for R. The process of using
virtualenv with Python

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:22.550
is really, really similar.

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:23.643
Really similar.

00:30:23.643 --> 00:30:25.310
If you want more
information about this,

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:28.190
we have website
environments.rstudio.com,

00:30:28.190 --> 00:30:32.613
where you can feel free to check
out more information on this.

00:30:32.613 --> 00:30:34.780
And if you're interested
in RStudio Package Manager,

00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:37.920
if that seems like something
that your team could use,

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:40.500
feel free to send us an
email at sales@rstudio.com,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:44.860
and we can talk
more with you there.

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:48.870
I'm going to pull up
the Slido questions.

00:30:51.710 --> 00:30:53.000
OK.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:55.640
"Also interested in
RStudio's docker images,

00:30:55.640 --> 00:30:59.300
but they are advertised
as experimental."

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:03.810
Any idea of when they will
be out of experimental?

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:04.610
No.

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:07.010
I don't know that we
have any plans to make

00:31:07.010 --> 00:31:10.040
them more strongly
supported than experimental

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.500
now, for the time being.

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:15.320
At this point, if
you want to use them,

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:17.600
you should feel
free to use them.

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:20.450
But no imminent plans
to stabilize them,

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:21.580
as far as I know.

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:26.250
Oh, this is a great question.

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:28.560
Somebody asks,
"Interested in the advice

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:31.930
that users should manage
their own libraries.

00:31:31.930 --> 00:31:33.870
To that end, what's
your advice on the role

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:35.718
of a centrally-managed
site library?"

00:31:35.718 --> 00:31:36.760
This is a great question.

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:39.270
I love this question.

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:46.320
This is a question that I
think a lot of IT folks have--

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:53.380
yeah, it's a really
good question.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:58.200
The short answer here is that I
do not believe it's necessary.

00:31:58.200 --> 00:31:59.550
It is definitely the case--

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:02.490
I strongly recommend
against trying

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:06.600
to disallow users from
being able to pull

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:08.490
their own packages, right?

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.770
Data scientists should
be able to manage

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:15.280
their own specific
packages for themselves.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:17.880
And the reason for that
is that, for example,

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:20.600
let's say that I
have just a centrally

00:32:20.600 --> 00:32:21.600
managed set of packages.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.940
Then somebody has to do
that management, right?

00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:26.250
Then IT has to manage
that library, which

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:28.740
can be very difficult in
terms of managing not only

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:31.890
what packages, but what
versions, what's installed,

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:33.310
what date was it installed?

00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:35.102
How does that correspond
to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:35.102 --> 00:32:37.360
gets really complicated
really, really fast.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:41.070
And so I strongly
recommend against having

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:44.430
all the packages in a
centrally site-managed library.

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:46.170
So there is another
version of this

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:47.753
that-- it's a little
bit softer, which

00:32:47.753 --> 00:32:50.820
is, an admin stands up a
starter set of packages

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:52.808
that are centrally installed.

00:32:52.808 --> 00:32:55.350
And then users can install their
own packages on top of that.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:57.810
But they can use the starter
set, if they want to,

00:32:57.810 --> 00:32:59.830
so that that works [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:02.700
And there's nothing
wrong with doing that.

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:04.080
It's totally fine.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:08.075
In most cases, it is
unnecessary, we tend to find.

00:33:08.075 --> 00:33:09.450
The big reasons
people want to do

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:16.890
this are because they want to
make things easier for data

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:19.140
scientists to get started.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.240
In general, folks can
install their own--

00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:24.250
if you can library a
package, you can install it.

00:33:24.250 --> 00:33:27.490
And if folks are having trouble
with system-level dependencies,

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:29.550
this is where we
recommend centralizing

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:31.410
those resources on
a server as opposed

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.990
to everybody working
on their own desktop.

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:36.240
So again, even on a
server, we recommend

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:39.570
everybody managing their own
individual libraries, not

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:42.000
a centrally managed
site library.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:43.555
So that's a great question.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:49.495
Here's another great question
on, "What does renv save?"

00:33:49.495 --> 00:33:51.960
Does it include all of the
packages that are installed

00:33:51.960 --> 00:33:53.835
or only those actually
used inside a project?

00:33:53.835 --> 00:33:55.590
Yeah, this is a great question.

00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:59.370
By default, if
you do a snapshot,

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:01.740
it snapshots only
those packages that

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:03.520
are in use at the current time.

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:05.520
And what that means is
that it can find them

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:11.940
somewhere in a library call
or a colon-colon call, right?

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:14.639
If it can find
either of those, it

00:34:14.639 --> 00:34:19.047
will capture those
as dependencies.

00:34:19.047 --> 00:34:20.880
This does sometimes get
people into trouble.

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:28.139
If people are doing
this, and then

00:34:28.139 --> 00:34:33.270
lapply lib
install.packages, this

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:35.400
will not get picked
up from renv and also

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:36.550
is a bad process, right?

00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:39.630
If you're doing this, this
is just a plan-- this says,

00:34:39.630 --> 00:34:42.025
you need renv.

00:34:42.025 --> 00:34:43.650
So if you're doing
this, don't do this.

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:44.556
Use renv instead.

00:34:44.556 --> 00:34:45.389
It works way better.

00:34:45.389 --> 00:34:47.880
It captures versions
and not just packages,

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:49.139
and also doesn't mess up--

00:34:49.139 --> 00:34:54.540
this could mess up somebody's
entire package environment.

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:57.690
There is a way, in renv, to turn
on auto snapshotting, which is,

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:02.370
basically, any package that gets
installed will get snapshotted.

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:08.590
And so that can end up
being more than the packages

00:35:08.590 --> 00:35:09.340
you actually need.

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:11.520
And then there is a
prune command, basically,

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:13.740
that allows you to prune
the renv library back

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:18.330
to only the things that
are actually being used.

00:35:18.330 --> 00:35:21.000
Great question.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:21.550
Let's see.

00:35:21.550 --> 00:35:23.550
"Do you have a free version
of Package Manager?"

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:25.290
Yes, it's the public one.

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.540
RStudio Package Manager--
packagemanager.rstudio.com--

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:29.010
is public Package Manager.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:31.200
If you want something
behind your firewall--

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:32.490
an internal version--

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:34.590
that is where our
pro product comes in.

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:35.640
So good question.

00:35:38.265 --> 00:35:40.890
"Package Manager provides a way
to control the list of packages

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:41.900
it caches.

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:44.090
When will it do the same
for Python packages?"

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:46.040
No guarantee on when.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:48.980
It is under active development
by the Package Manager team

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:50.200
and will be out shortly.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:54.770
For those of you who aren't
quite aware, Package Manager--

00:35:54.770 --> 00:36:00.828
the private version-- supports
Python repos, PyPI repos.

00:36:00.828 --> 00:36:02.870
Currently, though, it only
supports full mirrors.

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:05.250
It does not support subsets
and that sort of thing.

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:07.460
This will be forthcoming
in future versions

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:09.990
of Package Manager.

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:13.482
"What is your recommendation
to use a mix"--

00:36:13.482 --> 00:36:14.940
and this will be
the last question.

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:16.190
I think we're
running out of time.

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:17.648
"What is your
recommendation to use

00:36:17.648 --> 00:36:19.280
a mix of
validated/public packages

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:21.530
in one environment in a
highly regulated environment?"

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:24.390
Yeah, this is a great question.

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:27.150
So there are a bunch of
different ways to do this.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:30.695
The most common way
we see this done is--

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:36.460
I'm going to go back to-- this
is our internal demo version

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:38.590
of Package Manager--

00:36:38.590 --> 00:36:41.770
is, you can create a
validated repository here

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:43.780
inside RStudio Package Manager.

00:36:43.780 --> 00:36:45.090
And that can sit aside, right?

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:46.480
You can have multiple repos.

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:47.980
And so you can do this yourself.

00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:49.105
I'm showing you in
RStudio Package Manager,

00:36:49.105 --> 00:36:50.563
but you can do this
in other tools.

00:36:50.563 --> 00:36:53.090
This is not an RStudio Package
Manager [INAUDIBLE] thing,

00:36:53.090 --> 00:36:54.820
but I happen to have an
example here in RStudio Package

00:36:54.820 --> 00:36:55.408
Manager.

00:36:55.408 --> 00:36:56.950
You can create a
validated repository

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:59.290
that has only those validated
packages in it, right?

00:36:59.290 --> 00:37:02.878
And you can specify the
versions and the packages.

00:37:02.878 --> 00:37:04.420
And you can have
that sitting beside.

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:06.260
This is a full
CRAN mirror, right?

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:08.830
And so what you can do,
then, is you can say, OK,

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:11.870
for the validated projects,
use the validated repository,

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:12.370
right?

00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:14.510
And you, again, just go
to the Setup tab here.

00:37:14.510 --> 00:37:16.885
Make sure you're using the
appropriate binary repository.

00:37:16.885 --> 00:37:20.110
And if you want to, choose
a date, if you want to.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:24.280
And if you're doing a
more public project that

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:28.480
doesn't need that high
degree of validation,

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:31.450
just use the all-in
public repository.

00:37:31.450 --> 00:37:33.580
Or, the alternative
is, you could

00:37:33.580 --> 00:37:35.830
have the validated for
the validated repository

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:36.700
and the public--

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:39.160
you could just literally
use a public CRAN mirror.

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:42.190
This isn't quite as
ideal, because then, it's

00:37:42.190 --> 00:37:43.510
a little easier to mess up.

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:48.880
But that's the
recommendation there.

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:51.730
I know we did not have time to
get to all the questions here.

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:55.225
Unfortunately, we're,
I think, out of time.

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:01.970
I will be putting together some
more materials on this topic

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:03.702
and emailing them
out, so I really

00:38:03.702 --> 00:38:04.910
appreciate all the questions.

00:38:04.910 --> 00:38:06.680
I will take a look at
all these questions.

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:08.330
And we'll probably
include a little FAQ

00:38:08.330 --> 00:38:12.170
in some of the future
documents we send out.

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:16.165
And we'll try and address as
many of these as possible.

00:38:16.165 --> 00:38:18.290
Again, I really, really
appreciate everybody's time

00:38:18.290 --> 00:38:20.330
today and for sticking
with us through all

00:38:20.330 --> 00:38:21.830
the technical
difficulties today.

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:24.650
I've never quite had a
webinar go like this.

00:38:24.650 --> 00:38:27.200
But I really appreciate
everybody sticking with us

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:31.290
and hope you got something
out of the time today.

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:33.620
So thanks so much for
joining, and look forward

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:37.170
to hearing from you more soon.

